/-!
# The partition function is invisible to the modular flow, and to entropic-time differences

`kms-modular-catept.md` separates two claims that the three-clock presentation had run together, and
the separation matters because only one of them is `Z`-free.

**§8 of that note, the two candidate equalities.**

* *Generator/action equality*: `K_mod = Ŝ_I/ħ + log Z · 1`.
* *Clock-coordinate equality*: `s_mod = τ_ent = S_I/ħ`.

They are different claims, and the note is explicit that the first does not automatically imply the
second. The generator equality carries an additive `log Z · 1`; the clock-coordinate equality does
not, because a flow *parameter* has no scalar freedom to absorb.

**What is proved here.**  The `log Z · 1` in the generator equality is exactly a central shift, and
`QuantumInertialFrameModularGroup.unitaryFlow_conj_add_scalar` shows a central shift does not reach
the conjugation flow.  So:

> the modular flow generated by `Ŝ_I/ħ + log Z · 1` **is** the flow generated by `Ŝ_I/ħ`.

The partition function is therefore unobservable in the modular dynamics — the operator-level form
of §6's *"the partition function cancels"*.  Two consequences follow, and they cut in opposite
directions, which is the point:

* the generator equality is **insensitive** to `Z`, so it can never be used to determine `Z`, and any
  `Z` in it is pure gauge;
* the generator equality is correspondingly **weaker** than it looks: fixing the flow fixes the
  generator only up to `log Z · 1`, so a measurement of the flow cannot deliver `Ŝ_I` itself.

**§6, the state-side companion.**  The note's strongest formulation is relative:
`Δτ_ent = −log(p₂/p₁)`, in which `Z` cancels with no choice of zero.  §B proves the corresponding
invariance: the increment is unchanged when both probabilities are rescaled by a common factor.
That is the same common-mode rejection as `ComptonClock.PhaseLikeVisibilityDifferential` — the
absolute normalization is not the observable, the difference is.

Scope.  Both the generator and clock-coordinate equalities remain **hypotheses**: nothing here
establishes that `K_mod` is `Ŝ_I/ħ` or that `s_mod` is `τ_ent`.  What is settled is the *gauge
structure* — which parts of those claims are observable and which are not — and, per §8, that the
first does not deliver the second.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

noncomputable section

namespace Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.EntropicTime.ModularGeneratorPartitionFreedom

open QuantumMechanics.FiniteTarget

/-! ### §A — the partition function is gauge for the modular flow -/

variable {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H]
  [FiniteDimensional ℂ H]

/-- **`log Z` never reaches the modular flow.**  If the modular generator differs from the imaginary
action by the central term `log Z · 1` — the generator/action equality of `kms-modular-catept.md` §8
— then both generate the *same* conjugation flow.

This is the operator-level statement of §6's partition-function cancellation: `Z` is pure gauge for
the modular dynamics.  It cuts both ways: no observation of the modular flow can measure `Z`, and
equally, fixing the flow fixes the generator only up to `log Z · 1` — so §8's generator equality
cannot be read off the dynamics, and a measurement of the flow delivers `Ŝ_I` only modulo an
additive scalar. -/
theorem modularFlow_independent_of_logZ (S_I_hat : H →L[ℂ] H) (logZ : ℂ) (t : ℝ)
    (a : H →L[ℂ] H) :
    unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ • 1) t * a * unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ • 1) (-t)
      = unitaryFlow S_I_hat t * a * unitaryFlow S_I_hat (-t) :=
  unitaryFlow_conj_add_scalar S_I_hat logZ t a

/-- **Any two normalizations agree.**  Two candidate modular generators differing only by their
partition-function term generate the same flow, so no observation of the modular dynamics can
distinguish them — and in particular none can measure `Z`. -/
theorem modularFlow_normalization_indistinguishable (S_I_hat : H →L[ℂ] H) (logZ₁ logZ₂ : ℂ)
    (t : ℝ) (a : H →L[ℂ] H) :
    unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ₁ • 1) t * a * unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ₁ • 1) (-t)
      = unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ₂ • 1) t * a * unitaryFlow (S_I_hat + logZ₂ • 1) (-t) := by
  rw [modularFlow_independent_of_logZ, modularFlow_independent_of_logZ]

/-! ### §B — the state side: entropic-time increments are normalization-free -/

/-- The entropic-time increment between two histories, `Δτ_ent = −log(p₂/p₁)` — the relative
formulation of `kms-modular-catept.md` §6, in which the partition function cancels and no choice of
zero is needed. -/
def entropicTimeIncrement (p₁ p₂ : ℝ) : ℝ := -Real.log (p₂ / p₁)

/-- **A common rescaling cancels.**  Multiplying both history weights by the same positive factor —
which is what changing the normalization `Z` does — leaves the increment unchanged.  The absolute
weights are not the observable; their ratio is.

This is the state-side twin of §A, and the same common-mode rejection that
`ComptonClock.PhaseLikeVisibilityDifferential` proves for interference visibility. -/
theorem entropicTimeIncrement_scale_invariant {p₁ p₂ c : ℝ} (hp₁ : p₁ ≠ 0) (hc : c ≠ 0) :
    entropicTimeIncrement (c * p₁) (c * p₂) = entropicTimeIncrement p₁ p₂ := by
  unfold entropicTimeIncrement
  congr 2
  field_simp

/-- **Equal weights, no elapsed entropic time.**  The increment vanishes exactly on histories of
equal weight, so the clock advances only on a genuine likelihood difference. -/
theorem entropicTimeIncrement_self {p : ℝ} (hp : p ≠ 0) :
    entropicTimeIncrement p p = 0 := by
  unfold entropicTimeIncrement
  rw [div_self hp, Real.log_one, neg_zero]

/-- **Increments compose additively.**  Chaining two history comparisons adds their entropic-time
increments, so the relative clock is a genuine one-parameter accumulation and not merely a pairwise
label. -/
theorem entropicTimeIncrement_add {p₁ p₂ p₃ : ℝ} (h₁ : p₁ ≠ 0) (h₂ : p₂ ≠ 0) (h₃ : p₃ ≠ 0) :
    entropicTimeIncrement p₁ p₂ + entropicTimeIncrement p₂ p₃
      = entropicTimeIncrement p₁ p₃ := by
  unfold entropicTimeIncrement
  rw [← neg_add, ← Real.log_mul (div_ne_zero h₂ h₁) (div_ne_zero h₃ h₂)]
  congr 2
  field_simp

